        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(x)=-2(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)+x^2.$",
          "worked": "Let $Q(x)=P(x)-x^2$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}Q(1)&=1^2-1^2=0,\\\\Q(2)&=2^2-2^2=0,\\\\Q(3)&=3^2-3^2=0,\\\\Q(4)&=4-4^2=-12.\\end{align*}$$\nBy the Factor Theorem, $Q(x)=a(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)$. Using $Q(4)=-12$,\n$$\\begin{align*}a(4-1)(4-2)(4-3)&=-12,\\\\6a&=-12,\\\\a&=-2.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $P(x)=-2(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)+x^2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 58
      },
      {
        "id": "p28-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ of degree 3 for which $P(1)=2$, $P(2)=3$, $P(3)=4$ and $P(5)=12$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(x)=\\frac14(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)+x+1.$\n\nUse $Q(x)=P(x)-(x+1)$ to obtain roots $1,2,3$.",
          "worked": "Let $Q(x)=P(x)-(x+1)$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}Q(1)&=2-(1+1)=0,\\\\Q(2)&=3-(2+1)=0,\\\\Q(3)&=4-(3+1)=0,\\\\Q(5)&=12-(5+1)=6.\\end{align*}$$\nBy the Factor Theorem, $Q(x)=a(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)$. Using $Q(5)=6$,\n$$\\begin{align*}a(5-1)(5-2)(5-3)&=6,\\\\24a&=6,\\\\a&=\\frac14.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $P(x)=\\frac14(x-1)(x-2)(x-3)+x+1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 58
      }
    ]
  },
